3.Method Rule
-Precondition
-Postcondition

PreCondition
-A subclass method cannot require more restrictive conditions (inputs/state) than the superclass method. 
It can only keep them the same or make them looser (accept more inputs).

Precondition in Java
Meaning: A rule or check at the start of a method.
Purpose: Stops the method from running if the input data is bad.
How it looks: Usually written using if statements with IllegalArgumentException.

or you can use other third party libraries to check this:
Objects.requireNonNull();
import static com.google.common.base.Preconditions.checkArgument;

-PostCondition
A subclass method must guarantee at least as much as the superclass method (same or stricter outputs/state outcomes). It cannot weaken the guarantees.
A postcondition is a guarantee or rule that a method promises to make true when it finishes running successfully. 
A subclass should stregthen the superclass.

I-interface segration principal
The Interface Segregation Principle (ISP) states that a class should never be forced to implement an interface it does not use. 
Instead of creating one large, "fat" interface, you should break it down into multiple smaller, highly specific ones so that clients only know about the methods they actually care about.
Fat Interfaces: A single interface contains dozens of unrelated methods.
Empty Implementations: A class implements an interface but leaves several methods empty or returns null/throws an exception.
Unused Dependencies: Code depends on an entire large interface even though it only calls one specific method

D-Dependency Inversion Principle(ISP)
Dependency Inversion Principle- states that high-level modules should not depend on low-level modules, but both should depend on abstractions.
It also means abstractions should not depend on details, but details should depend on abstractions.
This design rule helps keep code flexible, easy to change, and simple to testI

Core Rules
High-level code (main business logic) and low-level code (database or network tools) must connect through a middle layer called an interface or abstraction.
No direct links: Your core logic should never talk directly to a specific tool or database. It only talks to the interface.
